---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL, SITE_NAMES } from '../../data/site-config';
import { srcLink } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Payment dates calendar Germany: publisher, sources, limits';
const description = 'Who publishes this calendar of German benefit payment dates: Radif Partners, independent, no loan offers. Where Kindergeld, pension and other dates come from.';
const s = (k: string, t: string) => srcLink(k, lang, t);
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1>About {SITE_NAMES[lang]}</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
    <p>Towards the end of each month, millions of households in Germany ask the same thing: when will the money arrive? The answers are scattered: Kindergeld dates with the Familienkasse, pension dates in a press release, the Grundsicherungsgeld rule in a statute, and many articles repeat a table without saying why a date falls on the 30th rather than the 31st. This site puts every date side by side, works them out for 2026 and 2027 and explains each shift caused by a weekend or holiday. If you receive several benefits, the home page lists your own next payment days and lets you add them to your calendar. English-speaking residents get the same information as German readers.</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The publisher is {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds tools from public rules and data. {AUTHOR_NAME} is not an authority, pays out no benefits, is not linked to any authority and uses no coats of arms or logos. The site offers no loans, no advances and no advice, and carries no affiliate links.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
    <p>Kindergeld dates come from the <Fragment set:html={s('ba-kg-termine', 'Familienkasse')} />, the 2026 pension dates from the <Fragment set:html={s('drv-termine', 'German Pension Insurance press release')} />. Every other date is worked out from the statute: <Fragment set:html={s('sgb6-118', 'section 118 SGB VI')} />, <Fragment set:html={s('sgb2-42', 'section 42 SGB II')} />, <Fragment set:html={s('sgb3-337', 'section 337 SGB III')} />, <Fragment set:html={s('beeg6', 'section 6 BEEG')} />, using the bank business day calendar and the <Fragment set:html={s('ecb-target', 'ECB TARGET closing days')} />. The <a href={route('method', lang)}>method</a> page explains how; the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a> explains how we correct.</p>
    <h2>Your data</h2>
    <p>All tools run in your browser. The benefits you tick, your last digit or your child's birth date are neither sent nor stored; the calendar file is also created on your device. There is no account and no form. Details in the <a href={route('privacy', lang)}>privacy policy</a>.</p>
    <h2>What the site does not do</h2>
    <p>It does not calculate amounts and does not know your award notice. Whether your first payment arrives on time, whether something is withheld or whether a back payment is due is for your office to answer.</p>
    <h2>Write to us</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> if a date is wrong.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
